    {
        "title" : "bicep-deploy vs bicep-action: A Tool or a Workflow?",
        "date_published" : "2026-11-16T00:00:00Z",
        "date_modified" : "2026-11-16T00:00:00Z",
        "id" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/bicep-deploy-vs-bicep-action-a-tool-or-a-workflow/",
        "url" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/bicep-deploy-vs-bicep-action-a-tool-or-a-workflow/",
        "summary": " Azure/bicep-deploy vs bicep-action: A Tool or a Workflow? I maintain innofactororg/bicep-action, so I should say up front that I am not a neutral party. I am also the person who most needs this comparison to be honest, because every time someone evaluates bicep-action for a new estate, the first question is the obvious one: why not just use Microsoft\u0026rsquo;s?\n",
        "author" : {
          "name" : "damian"
        },
        "content_html" : "\n\u003cp\u003e\n\n\n\n\n\n  \n  \n    \n  \n\n\n\u003cfigure class=\"my-8\"\u003e\n  \u003cimg src=\"/posts/bicep-deploy-vs-bicep-action-a-tool-or-a-workflow/bicep-deploy-vs-bicep-action/hero.png\" alt=\"A tool versus a workflow\" class=\"w-full rounded-lg\"  /\u003e\n  \n\u003c/figure\u003e\n\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eI maintain \u003ca href=\"https://github.com/innofactororg/bicep-action\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"\u003einnofactororg/bicep-action\u003c/a\u003e, so I should say up front that I am not a neutral party. I am also the person who most needs this comparison to be honest, because every time someone evaluates bicep-action for a new estate, the first question is the obvious one: why not just use Microsoft\u0026rsquo;s?\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/bicep-deploy-vs-bicep-action-a-tool-or-a-workflow/\"\u003eRead the full post on Incredible Technology, Incredibly Simple\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e",
        "language": "en"
    },
    {
        "title" : "bicep-action: Plan, Review and Deploy Bicep from Pull Requests",
        "date_published" : "2026-11-09T00:00:00Z",
        "date_modified" : "2026-11-09T00:00:00Z",
        "id" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/bicep-action-plan-review-and-deploy-bicep-from-pull-requests/",
        "url" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/bicep-action-plan-review-and-deploy-bicep-from-pull-requests/",
        "summary": " Terraform teams stopped deploying blind years ago. They open a pull request, a bot runs terraform plan and posts the result as a comment, a colleague reads exactly what will be created, changed and destroyed, and only then does anyone type atlantis apply. Most Bicep teams still merge and find out. The pipeline runs, the deployment succeeds or fails, and the first time anyone sees what Azure actually did is in the activity log afterwards.\n",
        "author" : {
          "name" : "damian"
        },
        "content_html" : "\u003cp\u003e\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\u003cfigure class=\"my-8\"\u003e\n  \u003cimg src=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/innofactororg/bicep-action/v1/docs/images/hero.png\" alt=\"Plan, review, deploy\" class=\"w-full rounded-lg\"  /\u003e\n  \n\u003c/figure\u003e\n\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eTerraform teams stopped deploying blind years ago. They open a pull request, a bot runs \u003ccode\u003eterraform plan\u003c/code\u003e and posts the result as a comment, a colleague reads exactly what will be created, changed and destroyed, and only then does anyone type \u003ccode\u003eatlantis apply\u003c/code\u003e. Most Bicep teams still merge and find out. The pipeline runs, the deployment succeeds or fails, and the first time anyone sees what Azure actually did is in the activity log afterwards.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/bicep-action-plan-review-and-deploy-bicep-from-pull-requests/\"\u003eRead the full post on Incredible Technology, Incredibly Simple\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e",
        "language": "en"
    },
    {
        "title" : "Azure Quota Groups: Recycling Capacity You Already Own",
        "date_published" : "2026-11-02T00:00:00Z",
        "date_modified" : "2026-11-02T00:00:00Z",
        "id" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/azure-quota-groups-recycling-capacity-you-already-own/",
        "url" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/azure-quota-groups-recycling-capacity-you-already-own/",
        "summary": "The most expensive vCPU in Azure is the one you already had approved, gave back by accident, and now have to ask for again. That is exactly what happens every time an old subscription is cancelled. Years of quota increases, each one a support ticket, a business justification and a few days of waiting, disappear with the subscription. Meanwhile the brand-new subscription that replaces it starts life with default limits, in a region where capacity is tight, and the first deployment into it fails with a quota error the platform team then has to chase.\n",
        "author" : {
          "name" : "damian"
        },
        "content_html" : "\u003cp\u003eThe most expensive vCPU in Azure is the one you already had approved, gave back by accident, and now have to ask for again. That is exactly what happens every time an old subscription is cancelled. Years of quota increases, each one a support ticket, a business justification and a few days of waiting, disappear with the subscription. Meanwhile the brand-new subscription that replaces it starts life with default limits, in a region where capacity is tight, and the first deployment into it fails with a quota error the platform team then has to chase.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/azure-quota-groups-recycling-capacity-you-already-own/\"\u003eRead the full post on Incredible Technology, Incredibly Simple\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e",
        "language": "en"
    },
    {
        "title" : "Azure DevOps Wiki to a Corporate PDF",
        "date_published" : "2026-10-05T00:00:00Z",
        "date_modified" : "2026-10-05T00:00:00Z",
        "id" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/from-azure-devops-wiki-to-a-corporate-pdf/",
        "url" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/from-azure-devops-wiki-to-a-corporate-pdf/",
        "summary": "The project documentation is in good shape. The team has been maintaining an Azure DevOps wiki, the architecture pages link to the detailed design, and the deployment instructions sit alongside the decisions that explain them.\nThen comes the handover request: “Can you send us the design document as a PDF? Please use the company template.”\nThat is a reasonable request. The recipient might not have access to Azure DevOps. An architecture board may need a fixed version to review, or the customer may need to file the document in a records system. A wiki is useful for the people doing the work; a controlled document is useful for the people accepting it.\n",
        "author" : {
          "name" : "damian"
        },
        "content_html" : "\u003cp\u003eThe project documentation is in good shape. The team has been maintaining an Azure DevOps wiki, the architecture pages link to the detailed design, and the deployment instructions sit alongside the decisions that explain them.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThen comes the handover request: “Can you send us the design document as a PDF? Please use the company template.”\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThat is a reasonable request. The recipient might not have access to Azure DevOps. An architecture board may need a fixed version to review, or the customer may need to file the document in a records system. A wiki is useful for the people doing the work; a controlled document is useful for the people accepting it.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/from-azure-devops-wiki-to-a-corporate-pdf/\"\u003eRead the full post on Incredible Technology, Incredibly Simple\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e",
        "language": "en"
    },
    {
        "title" : "Running Tailscale on a Ubiquiti EdgeRouter X",
        "date_published" : "2024-12-14T00:00:00Z",
        "date_modified" : "2024-12-14T00:00:00Z",
        "id" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/running-tailscale-on-a-ubiquiti-edgerouter/",
        "url" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/running-tailscale-on-a-ubiquiti-edgerouter/",
        "summary": " Running Tailscale on a Ubiquiti EdgeRouter Having grown up in the world of \u0026lsquo;on premise\u0026rsquo; computing, I have always prioritized data privacy in my home network.\nThough there may not be much of interest to hackers, there is still personal data and services that I want to keep secure. I have spent years working with OpenVPN and IPSec, building and supporting a Dynamic Multi-site VPN Mesh with Cisco DMVPN, and I know first hand the challenges of connecting clients to a private network and managing keys.\n",
        "author" : {
          "name" : "damian"
        },
        "content_html" : "\n\u003cp\u003eHaving grown up in the world of \u0026lsquo;on premise\u0026rsquo; computing, I have always prioritized data privacy in my home network.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThough there may not be much of interest to hackers, there is still personal data and services that I want to keep secure. I have spent years working with OpenVPN and IPSec, building and supporting a Dynamic Multi-site VPN Mesh with Cisco DMVPN, and I know first hand the challenges of connecting clients to a private network and managing keys.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/running-tailscale-on-a-ubiquiti-edgerouter/\"\u003eRead the full post on Incredible Technology, Incredibly Simple\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e",
        "language": "en"
    },
    {
        "title" : "Terraform or Bicep for Azure: Strengths Compared",
        "date_published" : "2023-02-15T00:00:00Z",
        "date_modified" : "2023-02-15T00:00:00Z",
        "id" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/considering-terraform-and-bicep/",
        "url" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/considering-terraform-and-bicep/",
        "summary": " Considering Terraform and Bicep As more organizations adopt cloud infrastructure, the demand for infrastructure as code (IAC) solutions has grown. Infrastructure as code allows developers to manage and automate infrastructure deployments using code, making it easier to track changes and maintain consistency.\nTerraform and Bicep are two popular IAC solutions that enable developers to manage and automate infrastructure deployments using code.\n",
        "author" : {
          "name" : "damian"
        },
        "content_html" : "\n\u003cp\u003eAs more organizations adopt cloud infrastructure, the demand for infrastructure as code (IAC) solutions has grown. Infrastructure as code allows developers to manage and automate infrastructure deployments using code, making it easier to track changes and maintain consistency.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eTerraform and Bicep are two popular IAC solutions that enable developers to manage and automate infrastructure deployments using code.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/considering-terraform-and-bicep/\"\u003eRead the full post on Incredible Technology, Incredibly Simple\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e",
        "language": "en"
    },
    {
        "title" : "Theming Hugo with a Hugo Module",
        "date_published" : "2022-11-21T00:00:00Z",
        "date_modified" : "2022-11-21T00:00:00Z",
        "id" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/theming-hugo-with-module/",
        "url" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/theming-hugo-with-module/",
        "summary": " Theming Hugo with Module To utilize modules in Hugo for our theme, we first must ensure that our site supports Hugo Modules.\nYou can quickly verify that you site is configured for Hugo Modules, as it should contain a go.mod file in the repository root.\nImport the theme module The concept of a Hugo theme has been superseded with the concept of modules, with the main difference that the former will allow you to build your site entirely, while the latter might implement only some modular features like enabling the ATOM feed, or adding a search to your website.\n",
        "author" : {
          "name" : "damian"
        },
        "content_html" : "\n\u003cp\u003eTo utilize modules in Hugo for our theme, we first must ensure that our site supports Hugo Modules.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cblockquote class=\"blockquote\"\u003e\n    \u003cp\u003eYou can quickly verify that you site is configured for Hugo Modules, as it should contain a  \u003ccode\u003ego.mod\u003c/code\u003e file in the repository root.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003c/blockquote\u003e\u003ch2 class=\"relative text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-secondary-500 mb-2 mt-12 -ml-px\"\u003e\n  \u003cdiv id=\"import-the-theme-module\" class=\"absolute -top-28\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\n  Import the \u003ccode\u003etheme\u003c/code\u003e module\n\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe concept of a Hugo \u003cem\u003etheme\u003c/em\u003e has been superseded with the concept of \u003cem\u003emodules\u003c/em\u003e, with the main difference that the former will allow you to build your site entirely, while the latter might implement only some modular features like enabling the ATOM feed, or adding a search to your website.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/theming-hugo-with-module/\"\u003eRead the full post on Incredible Technology, Incredibly Simple\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e",
        "language": "en"
    },
    {
        "title" : "Network Change Detection with Oxidized and Git",
        "date_published" : "2022-11-17T00:00:00Z",
        "date_modified" : "2022-11-17T00:00:00Z",
        "id" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/change-detection-using-oxidized/",
        "url" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/change-detection-using-oxidized/",
        "summary": "The process is very simple:\nLogin to each device in the router list router.db, Run Commands to get the information that will be saved Clean the output Commit the Changes to GIT Repository The tool is coded in Ruby, and implements a Domain Specific Language (DSL) for interaction.\nFinally, there is a Web based User experience included in the solution so we can get a fast overview of the world.\n",
        "author" : {
          "name" : "damian"
        },
        "content_html" : "\u003cp\u003eThe process is very simple:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003col\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eLogin to each device in the router list \u003ccode\u003erouter.db\u003c/code\u003e,\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eRun Commands to get the information that will be saved\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eClean the output\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eCommit the Changes to GIT Repository\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ol\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe tool is coded in \u003cem\u003eRuby\u003c/em\u003e, and implements a Domain Specific Language (DSL) for interaction.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eFinally, there is a Web based User experience included in the solution so we can get a fast overview of the world.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/change-detection-using-oxidized/\"\u003eRead the full post on Incredible Technology, Incredibly Simple\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e",
        "language": "en"
    },
    {
        "title" : "Obsidian to Hugo with Obsidian2Hugo",
        "date_published" : "2022-11-07T00:00:00Z",
        "date_modified" : "2022-11-07T00:00:00Z",
        "id" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/obsidian2hugo/",
        "url" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/obsidian2hugo/",
        "summary": "My Blog has evolved over the years, initially hosted on Wordpress, but some 6 years back I abandond that plaform for Static Sites, initially with Jekyll and used Github Pages, but that also got sunset in favor of my current generator Hugo.\nAs part of my current workflow, I use Obsidian for almost all my notes, which includes the posts which you get to read publically on this blog.\nTo make this happen, there are some very cool open source projects which I am relying to pipeline the content for its raw source, to the presentation which is finally published.\n",
        "author" : {
          "name" : "damian"
        },
        "content_html" : "\u003cp\u003eMy Blog has evolved over the years, initially hosted on Wordpress, but some 6 years back I abandond that plaform for Static Sites, initially with Jekyll and used Github Pages, but that also got sunset in favor of my current generator Hugo.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAs part of my current workflow, I use Obsidian for almost all my notes, which includes the posts which you get to read publically on this blog.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eTo make this happen, there are some very cool open source projects which I am relying to pipeline the content for its raw source, to the presentation which is finally published.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/obsidian2hugo/\"\u003eRead the full post on Incredible Technology, Incredibly Simple\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e",
        "language": "en"
    },
    {
        "title" : "Active Directory Administrative Limits: Finding and Resolving the Bloat",
        "date_published" : "2021-07-27T00:00:00Z",
        "date_modified" : "2021-07-27T00:00:00Z",
        "id" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/ad-administrative-limits/",
        "url" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/ad-administrative-limits/",
        "summary": "I recently ran into an issue with a particular environment where Active Directory and PKI services were deployed. One of the service accounts which I was attempting to \u0026lsquo;unlock\u0026rsquo; refused to co-operate and instead offered the most unhelpful message.\nAdministrative limit for this request was exceeded - this was not my first time encountering the message, previously this haunted me while I was managing a Windows PKI infrastructure and with some quick searches, confirmed my initial suspicion. This is a symptom of an AD object quite simply being to large!\n",
        "author" : {
          "name" : "damian"
        },
        "content_html" : "\u003cp\u003eI recently ran into an issue with a particular environment where Active Directory and PKI services were deployed. One of the service accounts which I was attempting to \u0026lsquo;unlock\u0026rsquo; refused to co-operate and instead offered the most unhelpful message.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ccode\u003eAdministrative limit for this request was exceeded\u003c/code\u003e - this was not my first time encountering the message, previously this haunted me while I was managing a Windows PKI infrastructure and with some quick searches, confirmed my initial suspicion. This is a symptom of an AD object quite simply being to large!\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/ad-administrative-limits/\"\u003eRead the full post on Incredible Technology, Incredibly Simple\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e",
        "language": "en"
    },
    {
        "title" : "FreeRADIUS for 802.1X with Certificates: EAP and PKI Setup",
        "date_published" : "2021-03-07T00:00:00Z",
        "date_modified" : "2021-03-07T00:00:00Z",
        "id" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/configuring-freeradius-for-802-1x/",
        "url" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/configuring-freeradius-for-802-1x/",
        "summary": "In a pervious post, I covered the steps to deploy the current stable release of FreeRadius, by installing the packages from the NetworkRadius organizations repository.\nWe will now, focus on configuring the FreeRadius server so that it can authenticate 802.1x requests, which depends on valid [Computer Certificates](/posts/create-pki-certificate-for-linux-server/#Creating our Certificate Request).\nWhy? Why would we go to this trouble, especially given the fact that Windows has shipped with its own NPS service, for the past 20 years?\n",
        "author" : {
          "name" : "damian"
        },
        "content_html" : "\u003cp\u003eIn a pervious post, I covered the steps to \u003ca href=\"/posts/installing-freeradius/\" rel=\"\"\u003edeploy the current stable release of FreeRadius, by installing the packages from the NetworkRadius organizations repository\u003c/a\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWe will now, focus on configuring the FreeRadius server so that it can authenticate 802.1x requests, which depends on valid [Computer Certificates](/posts/create-pki-certificate-for-linux-server/#Creating our Certificate Request).\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch2 class=\"relative text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-secondary-500 mb-2 mt-12 -ml-px\"\u003e\n  \u003cdiv id=\"why\" class=\"absolute -top-28\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\n  Why?\n\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhy would we go to this trouble, especially given the fact that Windows has shipped with its own NPS service, for the past 20 years?\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/configuring-freeradius-for-802-1x/\"\u003eRead the full post on Incredible Technology, Incredibly Simple\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e",
        "language": "en"
    },
    {
        "title" : "Extract Public and Private Keys from a PFX with OpenSSL",
        "date_published" : "2021-03-05T00:00:00Z",
        "date_modified" : "2021-03-05T00:00:00Z",
        "id" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/extracting-certificates-from-a-pfx/",
        "url" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/extracting-certificates-from-a-pfx/",
        "summary": " OpenSSL seperating a PFX into Public and Private Key Files Using OpenSSL we can extract the private key, and the certificate into independent file\u0026rsquo;s, which is required for most networking devices, and linux services.\nYou will need to install the OpenSSL, package, either on your system.\nExport the private key from the PFX file We begin, by passing in the PFX and requesting the Private key to be placed into its own file. This process will require that you provide the password which is used to protect the PFX file, and also a new password to protect the new private key file (this is not optional).\n",
        "author" : {
          "name" : "damian"
        },
        "content_html" : "\n\u003cp\u003eUsing \u003cstrong\u003eOpenSSL\u003c/strong\u003e we can extract the private key, and the certificate into independent file\u0026rsquo;s, which is required for most networking devices, and linux services.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eYou will need to install the \u003ca href=\"/posts/installing-the-latest-openssl-release/\" rel=\"\"\u003eOpenSSL\u003c/a\u003e, package, either on your system.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch2 class=\"relative text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-secondary-500 mb-2 mt-12 -ml-px\"\u003e\n  \u003cdiv id=\"export-the-private-key-from-the-pfx-file\" class=\"absolute -top-28\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\n  Export the private key from the PFX file\n\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWe begin, by passing in the PFX and requesting the Private key to be placed into its own file. This process will require that you provide the password which is used to protect the PFX file, and also a new password to protect the new private key file (this is not optional).\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/extracting-certificates-from-a-pfx/\"\u003eRead the full post on Incredible Technology, Incredibly Simple\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e",
        "language": "en"
    },
    {
        "title" : "Export a PFX from the Windows Certificate Store (MMC and PowerShell)",
        "date_published" : "2021-03-04T00:00:00Z",
        "date_modified" : "2021-03-04T00:00:00Z",
        "id" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/creating-a-pfx-from-a-certificate-in-windows/",
        "url" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/creating-a-pfx-from-a-certificate-in-windows/",
        "summary": " Exporting Windows Certificates to PFX A .pfx file is in essence an archive which can contain multiple objects, and can also be password protected; The format of this file is known as PKCS#12\nTypically, a .pfx usually contains one or more certificate, typically the chain of upstream authorities, and the corresponding private key. The most common usage of a PFX file is simplify certificate distribution to alternate systems or deployed to services.\n",
        "author" : {
          "name" : "damian"
        },
        "content_html" : "\n\u003cp\u003eA \u003ccode\u003e.pfx\u003c/code\u003e file is in essence an archive which can contain multiple objects, and can also be password protected; The format of this file is known as \u003cem\u003ePKCS#12\u003c/em\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eTypically, a \u003ccode\u003e.pfx\u003c/code\u003e usually contains one or more certificate, typically the chain of upstream authorities, and the corresponding private key. The most common usage of a PFX file is simplify certificate distribution to alternate systems or deployed to services.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/creating-a-pfx-from-a-certificate-in-windows/\"\u003eRead the full post on Incredible Technology, Incredibly Simple\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e",
        "language": "en"
    },
    {
        "title" : "Issue a Windows PKI Certificate to a Linux Server with OpenSSL",
        "date_published" : "2021-03-03T00:00:00Z",
        "date_modified" : "2021-03-03T00:00:00Z",
        "id" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/create-pki-certificate-for-linux-server/",
        "url" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/create-pki-certificate-for-linux-server/",
        "summary": " Issuing a Certificate using OpenSSL It is enevitable, that at some point in time, you will need to issue a certificate for your system. There are a couple of relativly simple steps to acheive this, begining with the creation of a certificate request, then submitting this to an online certificate authority, which will process the request and issue you a certificate with both a Public and Private Key.\n",
        "author" : {
          "name" : "damian"
        },
        "content_html" : "\n\u003cp\u003eIt is enevitable, that at some point in time, you will need to issue a certificate for your system. There are a couple of relativly simple steps to acheive this, begining with the creation of a certificate request, then submitting this to an online certificate authority, which will process the request and issue you a certificate with both a Public and Private Key.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/create-pki-certificate-for-linux-server/\"\u003eRead the full post on Incredible Technology, Incredibly Simple\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e",
        "language": "en"
    },
    {
        "title" : "Build and Install the Latest OpenSSL on Ubuntu",
        "date_published" : "2021-03-02T00:00:00Z",
        "date_modified" : "2021-03-02T00:00:00Z",
        "id" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/installing-the-latest-openssl-release/",
        "url" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/installing-the-latest-openssl-release/",
        "summary": " Installing the Latest OpenSSL release on Ubuntu Being over 25 years old, OpenSSL can be found on just about any system you work with today; but this does not imply that the version installed is current (or even close).\nDuring its life, there have been many instances where OpenSSL has been in the news, where some new vulnerability has being discovered, and quickly after, patched.\n",
        "author" : {
          "name" : "damian"
        },
        "content_html" : "\n\u003cp\u003eBeing over 25 years old, OpenSSL can be found on just about any system you work with today; but this does not imply that the version installed is current (or even close).\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eDuring its life, there have been many instances where OpenSSL has been in the news, where some new vulnerability has being discovered, and quickly after, patched.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/installing-the-latest-openssl-release/\"\u003eRead the full post on Incredible Technology, Incredibly Simple\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e",
        "language": "en"
    },
    {
        "title" : "Install FreeRADIUS on Ubuntu from the Network RADIUS Repository",
        "date_published" : "2021-03-01T00:00:00Z",
        "date_modified" : "2021-03-01T00:00:00Z",
        "id" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/installing-freeradius/",
        "url" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/installing-freeradius/",
        "summary": " FreeRADIUS Getting Started FreeRADIUS is an open source, high-performance, modular, scalable and feature-rich RADIUS server. It ships with both server and radius client, development libraries and numerous additional RADIUS related utilities, for Linux\nFreeRADIUS supports request proxying, with fail-over and load balancing, as well as the ability to access many types of back-end databases.\nRADIUS, which stands for Remote Authentication Dial-In User Service, is a network protocol used for remote user authentication and accounting. It provides AAA services; namely Authorization, Authentication, and Accounting.\n",
        "author" : {
          "name" : "damian"
        },
        "content_html" : "\n\u003cp\u003eFreeRADIUS is an open source, high-performance, modular, scalable and feature-rich RADIUS server. It ships with both server and radius client, development libraries and numerous additional RADIUS related utilities, for Linux\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eFreeRADIUS supports request proxying, with fail-over and load balancing, as well as the ability to access many types of back-end databases.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eRADIUS, which stands for \u003cem\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eR\u003c/strong\u003eemote \u003cstrong\u003eA\u003c/strong\u003euthentication \u003cstrong\u003eD\u003c/strong\u003eial-\u003cstrong\u003eI\u003c/strong\u003en \u003cstrong\u003eU\u003c/strong\u003eser \u003cstrong\u003eS\u003c/strong\u003eervice\u003c/em\u003e, is a network protocol used for remote user authentication and accounting. It provides AAA services; namely \u003cstrong\u003eA\u003c/strong\u003euthorization, \u003cstrong\u003eA\u003c/strong\u003euthentication, and \u003cstrong\u003eA\u003c/strong\u003eccounting.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/installing-freeradius/\"\u003eRead the full post on Incredible Technology, Incredibly Simple\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e",
        "language": "en"
    },
    {
        "title" : "Streaming Vinyl to Sonos with a Raspberry Pi and Icecast",
        "date_published" : "2021-02-21T00:00:00Z",
        "date_modified" : "2021-02-21T00:00:00Z",
        "id" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/streaming-vynil-on-sonos/",
        "url" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/streaming-vynil-on-sonos/",
        "summary": " Streaming Music from USB Turntable A little known trivia - I was once a DJ, and spent a lot of my youth behind the decks, in clubs around the West Of Ireland. Today, I still am the proud owner of a very large collection of Vynil and CD music, which of course deserves to get a second life with my digital streaming audio system powered by Sonos\n",
        "author" : {
          "name" : "damian"
        },
        "content_html" : "\n\u003cp\u003eA little known trivia - I was once a DJ, and spent a lot of my youth behind the decks, in clubs around the West Of Ireland. Today, I still am the proud owner of a very large collection of Vynil and CD music, which of course deserves to get a second life with my digital streaming audio system powered by Sonos\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/streaming-vynil-on-sonos/\"\u003eRead the full post on Incredible Technology, Incredibly Simple\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e",
        "language": "en"
    },
    {
        "title" : "Azure Tags as ARM Template Parameters",
        "date_published" : "2021-01-17T00:00:00Z",
        "date_modified" : "2021-01-17T00:00:00Z",
        "id" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/azure-iac-tags-as-parameters/",
        "url" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/azure-iac-tags-as-parameters/",
        "summary": " Azure as a State Machine, dogfooding Tags In the post, I am going to introduce a concept which will allow you to greatly up your Infrastructure as Code game, by using Azure as a State Machine!\nOne of the typical challenges when deploying ARM templates, is the sheer number of parameters which we find as a requirement to complete a deployment; which as you will appreciate gets considerably harder as we target many environments.\n",
        "author" : {
          "name" : "damian"
        },
        "content_html" : "\n\u003cp\u003eIn the post, I am going to introduce a concept which will allow you to greatly up your Infrastructure as Code game, by using Azure as a State Machine!\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eOne of the typical challenges when deploying ARM templates, is the sheer number of parameters which we find as a requirement to complete a deployment; which as you will appreciate gets considerably harder as we target many environments.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/azure-iac-tags-as-parameters/\"\u003eRead the full post on Incredible Technology, Incredibly Simple\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e",
        "language": "en"
    },
    {
        "title" : "Azure Enterprise Agreement: Delegating Roles for Subscription Creation",
        "date_published" : "2020-04-24T00:00:00Z",
        "date_modified" : "2020-04-24T00:00:00Z",
        "id" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/azure-ea-delegating-privileges/",
        "url" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/azure-ea-delegating-privileges/",
        "summary": " Azure Enterprise Agreement, Privilages Delegating Under the Enterprise agreement we have some different Persona\u0026rsquo;s, which have quite different abilities and operations upon which they are permitted to preform.\nBilling account simplifies billing for your organization while providing you enhanced billing and cost management capabilities. The following diagram explains how billing is organized in the new billing account.\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cost-management-billing/manage/mca-setup-account\nRoles and Accounts Before we being the process of delegation, It is important to understand this Hierarchy, so we can correctly proceed with the technical work ahead.\n",
        "author" : {
          "name" : "damian"
        },
        "content_html" : "\n\u003cp\u003eUnder the Enterprise agreement we have some different Persona\u0026rsquo;s, which have quite different abilities and operations upon which they are permitted to preform.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eBilling account simplifies billing for your organization while providing you enhanced billing and cost management capabilities. The following diagram explains how billing is organized in the new billing account.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cost-management-billing/manage/mca-setup-account\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"\u003ehttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cost-management-billing/manage/mca-setup-account\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\n\n\n\n\n\n  \n  \n    \n  \n\n\n\u003cfigure class=\"my-8\"\u003e\n  \u003cimg src=\"/posts/azure-ea-delegating-privileges/az-billing-ea_privilege_delegation.new_structure.png\" alt=\"\" class=\"w-full rounded-lg\"  /\u003e\n  \n\u003c/figure\u003e\n\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch2 class=\"relative text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-secondary-500 mb-2 mt-12 -ml-px\"\u003e\n  \u003cdiv id=\"roles-and-accounts\" class=\"absolute -top-28\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\n  Roles and Accounts\n\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eBefore we being the process of delegation, It is important to understand this Hierarchy, so we can correctly proceed with the technical work ahead.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/azure-ea-delegating-privileges/\"\u003eRead the full post on Incredible Technology, Incredibly Simple\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e",
        "language": "en"
    },
    {
        "title" : "Appending Tags to Azure Resources with ARM Templates",
        "date_published" : "2020-04-01T00:00:00Z",
        "date_modified" : "2020-04-01T00:00:00Z",
        "id" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/azure-iac-appending-tags/",
        "url" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/azure-iac-appending-tags/",
        "summary": "Todays conundrum: As I am leveraging templates, there will always be some standard tags I require to implement within the template, but I also require to provide additional tags as a parameter to be appended with the deployment.\nMy objective is to set up tags within an ARM template in accordance with good governance and the Cloud adoption framework.\nSolution ARM Template functions to the rescue. Todays salvation is called union, which you can learn more about on the actual [reference site][https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-resource-manager/resource-group-template-functions-array#union]\n",
        "author" : {
          "name" : "damian"
        },
        "content_html" : "\u003cp\u003eTodays conundrum: As I am leveraging templates, there will always be some standard tags I require to implement within the template, but I also require to provide additional tags as a parameter to be appended with the deployment.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eMy objective is to set up tags within an ARM template in accordance with good governance and the Cloud adoption framework.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch2 class=\"relative text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-secondary-500 mb-2 mt-12 -ml-px\"\u003e\n  \u003cdiv id=\"solution\" class=\"absolute -top-28\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\n  Solution\n\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eARM Template functions to the rescue. Todays salvation is called \u003ccode\u003eunion\u003c/code\u003e, which you can learn more about on the actual [reference site][https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-resource-manager/resource-group-template-functions-array#union]\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/azure-iac-appending-tags/\"\u003eRead the full post on Incredible Technology, Incredibly Simple\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e",
        "language": "en"
    },
    {
        "title" : "ZFS Primer: Disks, Pools, Redundancy and Health Checks",
        "date_published" : "2020-03-05T00:00:00Z",
        "date_modified" : "2020-03-05T00:00:00Z",
        "id" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/zfs-primer/",
        "url" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/zfs-primer/",
        "summary": "For years, my home lab ran on a Dell PowerEdge R620 and R510, both well supplied with disks and RAM. They were dependable machines and gave me plenty of room to learn about virtualisation, networking and the architecture behind Azure Stack.\nThey also had a second job. From my wife\u0026rsquo;s point of view, I\u0026rsquo;d installed a very elaborate airing cupboard. The room stayed warm enough to dry clothes, while the server fans did an excellent job of collecting the resulting fluff.\n",
        "author" : {
          "name" : "damian"
        },
        "content_html" : "\u003cp\u003eFor years, my home lab ran on a Dell PowerEdge R620 and R510, both well supplied with disks and RAM. They were dependable machines and gave me plenty of room to learn about virtualisation, networking and the architecture behind Azure Stack.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThey also had a second job. From my wife\u0026rsquo;s point of view, I\u0026rsquo;d installed a very elaborate airing cupboard. The room stayed warm enough to dry clothes, while the server fans did an excellent job of collecting the resulting fluff.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/zfs-primer/\"\u003eRead the full post on Incredible Technology, Incredibly Simple\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e",
        "language": "en"
    },
    {
        "title" : "Azure Application Gateway and WAF Updates (Ignite 2019)",
        "date_published" : "2019-11-07T00:00:00Z",
        "date_modified" : "2019-11-07T00:00:00Z",
        "id" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/ignite-2019-web-application-gateway-and-firewall/",
        "url" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/ignite-2019-web-application-gateway-and-firewall/",
        "summary": "Ignite Session: BRK3169 Presenter: Amit Srivastava\nMission Critical HTTP Applications, there are many things to consider\nPersonalized, Micro-Services, Rich Context\u0026hellip;. To support this MS have a number of services i the Suite - Azure Frontdoor, Application Gateway, Azure CDN, Web Application Firewall, Azure Load Balancer, and Azure Traffic Manager\nAzure Application Gateway Regional Gateway as a service\n",
        "author" : {
          "name" : "damian"
        },
        "content_html" : "\u003cp\u003eIgnite Session: BRK3169\nPresenter: Amit Srivastava\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eMission Critical HTTP Applications, there are many things to consider\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003ePersonalized, Micro-Services, Rich Context\u0026hellip;. To support this MS have a number of services i the Suite - Azure Frontdoor, Application Gateway, Azure CDN, Web Application Firewall, Azure Load Balancer, and Azure Traffic Manager\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eRegional Gateway as a service\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/ignite-2019-web-application-gateway-and-firewall/\"\u003eRead the full post on Incredible Technology, Incredibly Simple\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e",
        "language": "en"
    },
    {
        "title" : "Azure Private Link: Delivering Services Privately (Ignite 2019)",
        "date_published" : "2019-11-07T00:00:00Z",
        "date_modified" : "2019-11-07T00:00:00Z",
        "id" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/ignite-2019-private-link-delivering-services-privately/",
        "url" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/ignite-2019-private-link-delivering-services-privately/",
        "summary": " Azure Private Link MS Ignite 2019 Session: #BRK3168 Presenters: Narayan Annamalai and Sumeet Mittal\nThis service has exited Public Preview, and is now published to all regions as General Availability\nAdditionally today (November 2019) in US West Central, US North, and US East the ability to use with CosmosDB has been added to this service\n",
        "author" : {
          "name" : "damian"
        },
        "content_html" : "\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"/posts/microsoft-ignite-2019/\" rel=\"\"\u003eMS Ignite 2019\u003c/a\u003e Session: #BRK3168\nPresenters: Narayan Annamalai and Sumeet Mittal\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cblockquote class=\"blockquote\"\u003e\n    \u003cp\u003eThis service has exited Public Preview, and is now published to all regions as General Availability\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/blockquote\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cdel\u003eAdditionally today (November 2019) in US West Central, US North, and US East the ability to use with \u003cem\u003eCosmosDB\u003c/em\u003e has been added to this service\u003c/del\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/ignite-2019-private-link-delivering-services-privately/\"\u003eRead the full post on Incredible Technology, Incredibly Simple\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e",
        "language": "en"
    },
    {
        "title" : "C-Bus to MQTT Bridge on a Raspberry Pi with C-Gate",
        "date_published" : "2019-10-10T00:00:00Z",
        "date_modified" : "2019-10-10T00:00:00Z",
        "id" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/cbus-mqtt-bridge-on-raspberry-pi/",
        "url" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/cbus-mqtt-bridge-on-raspberry-pi/",
        "summary": " CBus MQTT Bridge on Raspberry PI Turn back to 2007; My wife and I built our home, integrating many smart technologies, including the Clipsal CBus lighting system. This solution is classified as a Prosumer technology, and is designed to integrate into whole house automation systems.\nThe CBus system implements however a proprietary technology, and utilizes a communication protocol which is not \u0026lsquo;open source\u0026rsquo;; however, accepting a license agreement will permit access to this protocol for creating an programming interface.\n",
        "author" : {
          "name" : "damian"
        },
        "content_html" : "\n\u003cp\u003eTurn back to 2007; My wife and I built our home, integrating many smart technologies, including the Clipsal CBus lighting system. This solution is classified as a Prosumer technology, and is designed to integrate into whole house automation systems.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe CBus system implements however a proprietary technology, and utilizes a communication protocol which is not \u0026lsquo;open source\u0026rsquo;; however, accepting a license agreement will permit access to this protocol for creating an programming interface.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/cbus-mqtt-bridge-on-raspberry-pi/\"\u003eRead the full post on Incredible Technology, Incredibly Simple\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e",
        "language": "en"
    },
    {
        "title" : "Configure the Azure Application Gateway WAF with PowerShell",
        "date_published" : "2019-10-03T00:00:00Z",
        "date_modified" : "2019-10-03T00:00:00Z",
        "id" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/configuring-the-web-application-firewall-with-powershell/",
        "url" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/configuring-the-web-application-firewall-with-powershell/",
        "summary": "Microsoft Azure Application Gateway is a Layer 7 application delivery controller (ADC) offered as a service in Azure. It provides load balancing, SSL termination, end-to-end SSL, URL path-based routing, and basic web application firewall (WAF) functionality.\nWorking with the WAF, I usually build a basic configuration in the Portal before exporting the ARM JSON, which, then becomes my primary method to working on this service.\nWhy JSON you may ask\u0026hellip; One of my biggest gripes with the Azure Firewall solutions currently is based on thier CRUD (Create, Read, Update and Delete) interface. It always results in a workflow from hell, training along the lines of \u0026lsquo;Edit, Save, WAIT,, Edit, Save, WAIT\u0026rsquo; in a painful loop. What should be a fast and straightforward configuration update, typically is a process that must be executed over many hours, preferably on a second screen.\n",
        "author" : {
          "name" : "damian"
        },
        "content_html" : "\u003cp\u003eMicrosoft Azure Application Gateway is a Layer 7 application delivery controller (ADC) offered as a service in Azure. It provides load balancing, SSL termination, end-to-end SSL, URL path-based routing, and basic web application firewall (WAF) functionality.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWorking with the WAF, I usually build a basic configuration in the Portal before exporting the ARM JSON, which, then becomes my primary method to working on this service.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch2 class=\"relative text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-secondary-500 mb-2 mt-12 -ml-px\"\u003e\n  \u003cdiv id=\"why-json-you-may-ask\" class=\"absolute -top-28\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\n  Why JSON you may ask\u0026hellip;\n\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eOne of my biggest gripes with the Azure Firewall solutions currently is based on thier CRUD \u003cem\u003e(Create, Read, Update and Delete)\u003c/em\u003e interface. It always  results in a workflow from hell, training along the lines of \u0026lsquo;\u003cem\u003eEdit, Save,\u003c/em\u003e \u003cem\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eWAIT,\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/em\u003e, \u003cem\u003eEdit, Save,\u003c/em\u003e \u003cem\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eWAIT\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/em\u003e\u0026rsquo; in a painful loop.  What should be a fast and straightforward configuration update, typically is a process that must be executed over many hours, preferably on a second screen.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/configuring-the-web-application-firewall-with-powershell/\"\u003eRead the full post on Incredible Technology, Incredibly Simple\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e",
        "language": "en"
    },
    {
        "title" : "Install Docker and Docker Compose on a Raspberry Pi (Raspbian Buster)",
        "date_published" : "2019-10-01T00:00:00Z",
        "date_modified" : "2019-10-01T00:00:00Z",
        "id" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/installing-docker-and-compose-on-raspbian-buster/",
        "url" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/installing-docker-and-compose-on-raspbian-buster/",
        "summary": "Quickly update a new Raspberry Pi, which has an install of Raspbian Buster with Docker and Docker-compose.\nDocker This is simple, as the Docker team have done all the work\nbash ShowHide 2 lines Copy code ⌄ curl -fsSL get.docker.com -o get-docker.sh sh get-docker.sh And, we can add our user to the Docker group so we do not need the sudo every time. I am using the environment variable $USER; which indicates who is logged in currently. In my case this is the user pi.\n",
        "author" : {
          "name" : "damian"
        },
        "content_html" : "\u003cp\u003eQuickly update a new Raspberry Pi, which has an install of Raspbian Buster with Docker and Docker-compose.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch2 class=\"relative text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-secondary-500 mb-2 mt-12 -ml-px\"\u003e\n  \u003cdiv id=\"docker\" class=\"absolute -top-28\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\n  Docker\n\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThis is simple, as the Docker team have done all the work\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block highlight show-line-numbers  my-2\"\u003e\n  \u003cdetails class=\"code-block-details\" open\u003e\n    \u003csummary class=\"code-block-title \"\u003e\n      \u003cspan class=\"code-block-label\"\u003ebash\u003c/span\u003e\n      \u003cspan class=\"code-block-toggle\"\u003e\u003cspan class=\"code-block-show\"\u003eShow\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"code-block-hide\"\u003eHide\u003c/span\u003e 2 lines\u003c/span\u003e\n      \u003cspan class=\"code-block-status\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\"\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\n      \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"code-block-copy\" data-code-target=\"codeblock-id-316f508ce637ffd096d16c1503bdef94-0\" hidden\u003eCopy code\u003c/button\u003e\n      \u003cspan class=\"code-block-chevron\" aria-hidden=\"true\"\u003e⌄\u003c/span\u003e\n    \u003c/summary\u003e\n    \u003cpre style=\"counter-reset: codeblock;\" class=\"block\"\u003e\u003ccode\n      id=\"codeblock-id-316f508ce637ffd096d16c1503bdef94-0\"\n      class=\"chroma block code-block-content ease-in-out\"\u003e\u003cspan class=\"line\"\u003e\u003cspan class=\"cl\"\u003ecurl -fsSL get.docker.com -o get-docker.sh\n\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"line\"\u003e\u003cspan class=\"cl\"\u003esh get-docker.sh\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n  \u003c/details\u003e\n\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAnd, we can add our user to the Docker group so we do not need the \u003ccode\u003esudo\u003c/code\u003e every time. I am using the environment variable \u003ccode\u003e$USER\u003c/code\u003e; which indicates who is logged in currently. In my case this is the user \u003cem\u003epi\u003c/em\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/installing-docker-and-compose-on-raspbian-buster/\"\u003eRead the full post on Incredible Technology, Incredibly Simple\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e",
        "language": "en"
    },
    {
        "title" : "Running FastLED on the Dual-Core ESP32",
        "date_published" : "2019-09-11T00:00:00Z",
        "date_modified" : "2019-09-11T00:00:00Z",
        "id" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/running-fastled-on-the-dual-core-esp32/",
        "url" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/running-fastled-on-the-dual-core-esp32/",
        "summary": " FastLED Package There are many projects posted over the web which implement the excellent FastLED library on the ESP12 processor; however locating a project which implements this on the more powerful sibling is a lot more difficult.\nSo, with a few failed attempts and a lot of patching samples together; I have a stable running implementation which you can clone or fork to get up and running quickly with your own projects.\n",
        "author" : {
          "name" : "damian"
        },
        "content_html" : "\u003ch2 class=\"relative text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-secondary-500 mb-2 mt-12 -ml-px\"\u003e\n  \u003cdiv id=\"fastled-package\" class=\"absolute -top-28\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\n  FastLED Package\n\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThere are many projects posted over the web which implement the excellent FastLED library on the ESP12 processor; however locating a project which implements this on the more powerful sibling is a lot more difficult.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eSo, with a few failed attempts and a lot of patching samples together; I have a stable running implementation which you can clone or fork to get up and running quickly with your own projects.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/running-fastled-on-the-dual-core-esp32/\"\u003eRead the full post on Incredible Technology, Incredibly Simple\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e",
        "language": "en"
    },
    {
        "title" : "WireGuard on a UniFi USG: Tunnels, Secrets and Firewall",
        "date_published" : "2019-08-17T00:00:00Z",
        "date_modified" : "2019-08-17T00:00:00Z",
        "id" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/configure-wireguard-on-unifi-usg/",
        "url" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/configure-wireguard-on-unifi-usg/",
        "summary": " Install the Wireguard Package SSH directly to your USG, and run the following commands:\nbash ShowHide 3 lines Copy code ⌄ curl -L https://github.com/Lochnair/vyatta-wireguard/releases/download/0.0.20190123/wireguard-ugw3-0.0.20190702-1.deb -o /tmp/wireguard.deb dpkg -i /tmp/wireguard.deb Create the Tunnel Secrets To keep stuff private, we will encrypt the traffic using a long password, known as a \u0026lsquo;Key\u0026rsquo;. To make sure this is unique, we will use a tool provided by Wireguard to make a random key for us.\n",
        "author" : {
          "name" : "damian"
        },
        "content_html" : "\u003ch2 class=\"relative text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-secondary-500 mb-2 mt-12 -ml-px\"\u003e\n  \u003cdiv id=\"install-the-wireguard-package\" class=\"absolute -top-28\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\n  Install the Wireguard Package\n\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eSSH directly to your USG, and run the following commands:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block highlight show-line-numbers  my-2\"\u003e\n  \u003cdetails class=\"code-block-details\" open\u003e\n    \u003csummary class=\"code-block-title \"\u003e\n      \u003cspan class=\"code-block-label\"\u003ebash\u003c/span\u003e\n      \u003cspan class=\"code-block-toggle\"\u003e\u003cspan class=\"code-block-show\"\u003eShow\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"code-block-hide\"\u003eHide\u003c/span\u003e 3 lines\u003c/span\u003e\n      \u003cspan class=\"code-block-status\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\"\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\n      \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"code-block-copy\" data-code-target=\"codeblock-id-ccd4937b7b1c7c00dfbc753f6a0349cd-0\" hidden\u003eCopy code\u003c/button\u003e\n      \u003cspan class=\"code-block-chevron\" aria-hidden=\"true\"\u003e⌄\u003c/span\u003e\n    \u003c/summary\u003e\n    \u003cpre style=\"counter-reset: codeblock;\" class=\"block\"\u003e\u003ccode\n      id=\"codeblock-id-ccd4937b7b1c7c00dfbc753f6a0349cd-0\"\n      class=\"chroma block code-block-content ease-in-out\"\u003e\u003cspan class=\"line\"\u003e\u003cspan class=\"cl\"\u003ecurl -L https://github.com/Lochnair/vyatta-wireguard/releases/download/0.0.20190123/wireguard-ugw3-0.0.20190702-1.deb -o /tmp/wireguard.deb\n\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"line\"\u003e\u003cspan class=\"cl\"\u003e\n\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"line\"\u003e\u003cspan class=\"cl\"\u003edpkg -i /tmp/wireguard.deb\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n  \u003c/details\u003e\n\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003ch3 class=\"text-xl font-bold text-secondary-500 leading-tight mb-2\" id=\"create-the-tunnel-secrets\"\u003e\u003ca href=\"#create-the-tunnel-secrets\" class=\"header-mark\" aria-label=\"Header mark for 'Create the Tunnel Secrets'\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\n    Create the Tunnel Secrets\n\u003c/h3\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eTo keep stuff private, we will encrypt the traffic using a long password, known as a \u0026lsquo;Key\u0026rsquo;. To make sure this is unique, we will use a tool provided by Wireguard to make a random key for us.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/configure-wireguard-on-unifi-usg/\"\u003eRead the full post on Incredible Technology, Incredibly Simple\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e",
        "language": "en"
    },
    {
        "title" : "Updating Pester on Windows 10: The Hard Way",
        "date_published" : "2019-01-29T00:00:00Z",
        "date_modified" : "2019-01-29T00:00:00Z",
        "id" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/updating-pester-on-windows-10/",
        "url" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/updating-pester-on-windows-10/",
        "summary": "I spend the majority of my time working on my Windows machines, and for many scenarios, I find it difficult to complain. However, when Windows decides to dig the boot in and not co-operative; usually is when I grab my Mac Book and get the work done.\nHowever, running aware from the problem rarely is a good fix for the issue; My latest battle has been Pester. The testing framework builds on Powershell, and by the grace of God, now shipped as part of the Windows 10 operating system.\n",
        "author" : {
          "name" : "damian"
        },
        "content_html" : "\u003cp\u003eI spend the majority of my time working on my Windows machines, and for many scenarios, I find it difficult to complain. However, when Windows decides to dig the boot in and not co-operative; usually is when I grab my Mac Book and get the work done.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, running aware from the problem rarely is a good fix for the issue; My latest battle has been Pester. The testing framework builds on Powershell, and by the grace of God, now shipped as part of the Windows 10 operating system.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/updating-pester-on-windows-10/\"\u003eRead the full post on Incredible Technology, Incredibly Simple\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e",
        "language": "en"
    },
    {
        "title" : "Starting Services at Boot on Linux: rc.local, init.d, systemd and cron",
        "date_published" : "2019-01-14T00:00:00Z",
        "date_modified" : "2019-01-14T00:00:00Z",
        "id" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/waking-deamons/",
        "url" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/waking-deamons/",
        "summary": "With a multitude of Raspberry PI\u0026rsquo;s deployed around the house, each taking a dedicated duty in ensuring that services run transparently; It is not uncommon for me to discover the initialization scripts designed to have these services auto start at boot is not working.\nThe content of this post is a reference for different methods which can be employed to resolve these stubborn daemons; which always are to fond of reappearing after an unplanned outage; or what is more commonly referred to as a Power Failure!\n",
        "author" : {
          "name" : "damian"
        },
        "content_html" : "\u003cp\u003eWith a multitude of Raspberry PI\u0026rsquo;s deployed around the house, each taking a dedicated duty in ensuring that services run transparently; It is not uncommon for me to discover the initialization scripts designed to have these services auto start at boot is not working.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe content of this post is a reference for different methods which can be employed to resolve these stubborn daemons; which always are to fond of reappearing after an unplanned outage; or what is more commonly referred to as a Power Failure!\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/waking-deamons/\"\u003eRead the full post on Incredible Technology, Incredibly Simple\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e",
        "language": "en"
    },
    {
        "title" : "Azure Cloud Shell in Visual Studio Code: Sign In and Mount the Drive",
        "date_published" : "2019-01-04T00:00:00Z",
        "date_modified" : "2019-01-04T00:00:00Z",
        "id" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/configure-vs-code-with-azure-cloud-shell/",
        "url" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/configure-vs-code-with-azure-cloud-shell/",
        "summary": "After years living in tools like Visual Studio, and PowerShell; Currently my primary landing ground is Visual Studio Code. With my target audience firmly defined as Azure; In this post I am going to share my notes on how to get these two tools working harmonisly; and to make the experience a little richer, we will also mount the underlying Cloud Drive File Share of the Azure Cloud Shell on our local computer as a PowerShell Drive (PSDrive).\n",
        "author" : {
          "name" : "damian"
        },
        "content_html" : "\u003cp\u003eAfter years living in tools like Visual Studio, and PowerShell; Currently my primary landing ground is Visual Studio Code. With my target audience firmly defined as Azure; In this post I am going to share my notes on how to get these two tools working harmonisly; and to make the experience a little richer, we will also mount the underlying Cloud Drive File Share of the Azure Cloud Shell on our local computer as a PowerShell Drive (PSDrive).\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/configure-vs-code-with-azure-cloud-shell/\"\u003eRead the full post on Incredible Technology, Incredibly Simple\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e",
        "language": "en"
    },
    {
        "title" : "Happy New Year 2019: Speaking, Azure and IoT",
        "date_published" : "2019-01-02T00:00:00Z",
        "date_modified" : "2019-01-02T00:00:00Z",
        "id" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/2019-happy-new-year/",
        "url" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/2019-happy-new-year/",
        "summary": "First, allow me to thank all of you that are continuing to read this blog; despite the drought which I am predicting has now passed. I have a long list of topics which I wish to address in detail throughout the year; focused on the areas which I am most passionate.\nSpeaking Last year I had the opportunity to meet a lot of you in various parts of the globe, primarily speaking at some significant events; including the \u0026lsquo;Nordic NIC Conference\u0026rsquo;, \u0026lsquo;Cloud and Datacenter Conference\u0026rsquo;, \u0026lsquo;Tech Camp\u0026rsquo; and \u0026lsquo;Experts Live\u0026rsquo;.\n",
        "author" : {
          "name" : "damian"
        },
        "content_html" : "\u003cp\u003eFirst, allow me to thank all of you that are continuing to read this blog; despite the drought which I am predicting has now passed. I have a long list of topics which I wish to address in detail throughout the year; focused on the areas which I am most passionate.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch2 class=\"relative text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-secondary-500 mb-2 mt-12 -ml-px\"\u003e\n  \u003cdiv id=\"speaking\" class=\"absolute -top-28\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\n  Speaking\n\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eLast year I had the opportunity to meet a lot of you in various parts of the globe, primarily speaking at some significant events; including the \u0026lsquo;Nordic NIC Conference\u0026rsquo;, \u0026lsquo;Cloud and Datacenter Conference\u0026rsquo;, \u0026lsquo;Tech Camp\u0026rsquo; and \u0026lsquo;Experts Live\u0026rsquo;.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/2019-happy-new-year/\"\u003eRead the full post on Incredible Technology, Incredibly Simple\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e",
        "language": "en"
    },
    {
        "title" : "Calendar Chaos: Syncing Family, Google and Work Calendars",
        "date_published" : "2018-12-18T00:00:00Z",
        "date_modified" : "2018-12-18T00:00:00Z",
        "id" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/assistants-familys-work-and-calendars-chaos/",
        "url" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/assistants-familys-work-and-calendars-chaos/",
        "summary": "Assumingly I am not alone, when we sit down as a family and talk about our day, and the plan for the next days or the weekend; only to realize that we have some real scheduling issues; because you totally forgot that you would take the children to an event; while your partner had a long-standing appointment with the hairdresser.\nLetting this happen once or twice, is forgivable, but happing on a regular basis; is the recipe for a lousy dispute; that we do not need.\n",
        "author" : {
          "name" : "damian"
        },
        "content_html" : "\u003cp\u003eAssumingly I am not alone, when we sit down as a family and talk about our day, and the plan for the next days or the weekend; only to realize that we have some real scheduling issues; because you totally forgot that you would take the children to an event; while your partner had a long-standing appointment with the hairdresser.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eLetting this happen once or twice, is forgivable, but happing on a regular basis; is the recipe for a lousy dispute; that we do not need.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/assistants-familys-work-and-calendars-chaos/\"\u003eRead the full post on Incredible Technology, Incredibly Simple\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e",
        "language": "en"
    },
    {
        "title" : "My Journey to the Internet of Things: ESP8266, Arduino and PlatformIO",
        "date_published" : "2018-12-06T00:00:00Z",
        "date_modified" : "2018-12-06T00:00:00Z",
        "id" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/my-journey-to-the-internet-of-things/",
        "url" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/my-journey-to-the-internet-of-things/",
        "summary": "In a previous post, I referred to an embedded device which is called a NodeMCU. This device is a developer kit, designed to make it easy to develop and test programs for the embedded ESP8266 System on a Chip. Many manufacturers are offering both developer and production kits which leverage this SoC; including the Wemos D1, Lolin, and in my case the NodeMCU.\nThe function of these developer kits is to add some supporting features, for example, a USB to serial converter which makes programming a lot easier, in addition to a couple of buttons, and maybe an LED or two, for testing some simple scenarios with.\n",
        "author" : {
          "name" : "damian"
        },
        "content_html" : "\u003cp\u003eIn a previous post, I referred to an embedded device which is called a \u003cstrong\u003eNodeMCU\u003c/strong\u003e. This device is a developer kit, designed to make it easy to develop and test programs for the embedded \u003cem\u003eESP8266 System on a Chip\u003c/em\u003e. Many manufacturers are offering both developer and production kits which leverage this SoC; including the Wemos D1, Lolin, and in my case the NodeMCU.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe function of these developer kits is to add some supporting features, for example, a USB to serial converter which makes programming a lot easier, in addition to a couple of buttons, and maybe an LED or two, for testing some simple scenarios with.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/my-journey-to-the-internet-of-things/\"\u003eRead the full post on Incredible Technology, Incredibly Simple\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e",
        "language": "en"
    },
    {
        "title" : "Azure Policy as Code: Escaping Quotes in ARM Templates",
        "date_published" : "2018-11-20T00:00:00Z",
        "date_modified" : "2018-11-20T00:00:00Z",
        "id" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/defining-policy-as-code-with-arm-templates/",
        "url" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/defining-policy-as-code-with-arm-templates/",
        "summary": "My colleagues and friends Tao Yang , and Stanislav Zhelyazkov have both recently posts interesting topics on how to implement your Azure Policy as Code which I strongly recommend you take a few moments to review\nUsing ARM Templates to deploy azure policy definitions that require input parameters Defining input parameters for policy definitions in ARM Templates Improving Readability Both of these topics address the core of the challenges we face when approaching policy as an Infrastructure as Code problem. However, one of the things that is lost in the translation is the readability of the templates which they are deploying.\n",
        "author" : {
          "name" : "damian"
        },
        "content_html" : "\u003cp\u003eMy colleagues and friends \u003ca href=\"https://blog.tyang.org/2018/06/06/using-arm-templates-to-deploying-azure-policy-definitions-that-requires-input-parameters/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"\u003eTao Yang\u003c/a\u003e , and \u003ca href=\"https://cloudadministrator.net/2018/07/17/defining-input-parameters-for-policy-definitions-in-arm-template/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"\u003eStanislav Zhelyazkov\u003c/a\u003e have both recently posts interesting topics on how to implement your Azure Policy as Code which I strongly recommend you take a few moments to review\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://blog.tyang.org/2018/06/06/using-arm-templates-to-deploying-azure-policy-definitions-that-requires-input-parameters/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"\u003eUsing ARM Templates to deploy azure policy definitions that require input parameters\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://cloudadministrator.net/2018/07/17/defining-input-parameters-for-policy-definitions-in-arm-template/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"\u003eDefining input parameters for policy definitions in ARM Templates\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e\n\u003ch2 class=\"relative text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-secondary-500 mb-2 mt-12 -ml-px\"\u003e\n  \u003cdiv id=\"improving-readability\" class=\"absolute -top-28\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\n  Improving Readability\n\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eBoth of these topics address the core of the challenges we face when approaching policy as an Infrastructure as Code problem. However, one of the things that is lost in the translation is the readability of the templates which they are  deploying.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/defining-policy-as-code-with-arm-templates/\"\u003eRead the full post on Incredible Technology, Incredibly Simple\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e",
        "language": "en"
    },
    {
        "title" : "Book Recommendation: WaveMaker",
        "date_published" : "2018-11-07T00:00:00Z",
        "date_modified" : "2018-11-07T00:00:00Z",
        "id" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/reading-material-wavemaker/",
        "url" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/reading-material-wavemaker/",
        "summary": "Careers are what we all invest our energy and emotions in, either positively or negatively. Positive being the belief that we can make significant progress in this or another organisation and that it will give us the wherewithal to have a happy and productive life. Negative in that we can feel that everyone is out to get us, and the slippery pole has been freshly greased to scupper us. That it’s a “not what you know but who you know” world, and, unfortunately, you don’t know anyone. That life’s a bitch and then you die!\n",
        "author" : {
          "name" : "damian"
        },
        "content_html" : "\u003cp\u003eCareers are what we all invest our energy and emotions in, either positively or negatively. Positive being the belief that we can make significant progress in this or another organisation and that it will give us the wherewithal to have a happy and productive life. Negative in that we can feel that everyone is out to get us, and the slippery pole has been freshly greased to scupper us. That it’s a “not what you know but who you know” world, and, unfortunately, you don’t know anyone. That life’s a bitch and then you die!\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/reading-material-wavemaker/\"\u003eRead the full post on Incredible Technology, Incredibly Simple\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e",
        "language": "en"
    },
    {
        "title" : "Static Comments for a Static Site: From WordPress and Disqus to Utterances",
        "date_published" : "2018-10-30T00:00:00Z",
        "date_modified" : "2018-10-30T00:00:00Z",
        "id" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/static-comments/",
        "url" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/static-comments/",
        "summary": "At this point we are almost ready to go live with our site, however, one of the cornerstones to growing and sharing is communication.\nWordpress In the world of Wordpress, this was a standard core feature, which leveraged the fact that the pages were rendered on demand from a backend database. In this scenario, the same approach is offered to maintain a commenting platform.\n",
        "author" : {
          "name" : "damian"
        },
        "content_html" : "\u003cp\u003eAt this point we are almost ready to go live with our site, however, one of the cornerstones to growing and sharing is communication.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch2 class=\"relative text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-secondary-500 mb-2 mt-12 -ml-px\"\u003e\n  \u003cdiv id=\"wordpress\" class=\"absolute -top-28\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\n  Wordpress\n\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn the world of Wordpress, this was a standard core feature, which leveraged the fact that the pages were rendered on demand from a backend database. In this scenario, the same approach is offered to maintain a commenting platform.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/static-comments/\"\u003eRead the full post on Incredible Technology, Incredibly Simple\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e",
        "language": "en"
    },
    {
        "title" : "Host a Static Website in Azure Blob Storage with CDN",
        "date_published" : "2018-10-20T00:00:00Z",
        "date_modified" : "2018-10-20T00:00:00Z",
        "id" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/hosting-static-sites-in-azure-with-cdn/",
        "url" : "https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/hosting-static-sites-in-azure-with-cdn/",
        "summary": "Hosting my site on Wordpress was not super complex; I leveraged the Azure PaaS Services for Web Apps, and orginally the 3rd party support for hosted MySQL database\u0026rsquo;s. Once I was up and running I quickly realised that all media hosted on the site were landing on the webserver, so a plugin from its marketplace offered the ability to relocate the media to an Azure Blob; offloading some of the challanges.\n",
        "author" : {
          "name" : "damian"
        },
        "content_html" : "\u003cp\u003eHosting my site on Wordpress was not super complex; I leveraged the Azure PaaS Services for Web Apps, and orginally the 3rd party support for hosted MySQL database\u0026rsquo;s. Once I was up and running I quickly realised that all media hosted on the site were landing on the webserver, so a plugin from its marketplace offered the ability to relocate the media to an Azure Blob; offloading some of the challanges.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://damianflynn.github.io/posts/hosting-static-sites-in-azure-with-cdn/\"\u003eRead the full post on Incredible Technology, Incredibly Simple\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e",
        "language": "en"
    },
